an attempt by china to secure a sweeping  security and trade deal with 10 south  pacific islands remains elusive chinese  foreign minister wang yi has was hoping  to sign such a pact during a stop in  fiji part of his diplomatic tour of the  region but juan came away from a virtual  meeting with his south pacific  counterparts without a deal he urged  them not to be too anxious about his  country's aims in the region and  promised to clear the air  after the meeting china will release its  own position paper on our own positions  propositions and cooperation proposals  with pacific island countries  and going forward we will continue to  have ongoing and in-depth discussions  and consultations to shape more  consensus on cooperation  but the leader to that commitment saw  charm offensive to get the pacific  nations on board one that set alarm  bells ringing in canberra but also as  you will see in our next report in some  pacific islands as well  a whistle-stop charm offensive by  china's foreign minister wang yi  he's spending 10 days island hopping in  the pacific  seeking to deepen beijing's regional  clout  at the centre of the tour a proposed  cooperation packed with 10 pacific  island nations  china is calling it a common development  vision  but a draft of the plan shows its  ambitions go much further  beijing will become more involved in  regional security  training police officers and expanding  its cyber  reach  it would also gain greater access to  natural resources and sensitive marine  mapping in return beijing's dangling the  carrot of a free trade deal along with  access to china's lucrative market  it's also offering financial assistance  including humanitarian and pandemic  relief  the deal would drastically increase  china's role in the politics security  and economy of the south pacific  that has countries like australia and  the us rattled  because of their geographical proximity  and long-standing strategic interests in  the region  the u.s state department has expressed  alarm saying beijing might use the  proposed accord to exploit the islands  and fuel regional tensions  concerns echoed by australia  ahead of monday's meeting micronesia's  president david panwello penned an  eight-page letter warning fellow pacific  island leaders that the agreement  threatens to bring a new cold war era at  best and a world war at worst  china's brushing off those fears  to the pacific island countries china is  not a newcomer but an old friend with a  long-standing friendship the rapid  progress in the relationship between  china and the pacific island countries  does not come out of thin air but has  naturally progressed over time  china's adamant that the deal has no  political strings attached  but with not all pacific island leaders  convinced it has more diplomatic blitzes  ahead to calm their fears  and joining me now for more is rory  metcalf he is head of the national  security college at the australian  national university in canberra rory  china hasn't been able to sign off on  its security and economic deal with the  10 pacific island states as it wanted to  why do you think that is  well this is a significant moment in  china's attempt to dominate the pacific  and i think china has overreached so i  think we've seen a  convergence of several factors in the  past few days the um the overreach the  the hubris if you like of  uh china and and foreign minister uh  wang yi the attempt to uh to to wrap  many countries into a mini-dimensional  agreement security  economics culture development fisheries  etc  and of course there was going to be  opposition so i think what's happening  now is that there's a divisiveness that  china is bringing to the region and the  cracks the divisions are beginning to  show  because the small island countries  cannot reach consensus on doing a deal  with china therefore the deal has been  put on the shelf but the other piece of  the puzzle i think is the way that the  new australian government has been  showing real leadership and real  engagement with the pacific island  countries not telling them to  refuse or ignore china but to remind  them that the choices that they are  making about their development are for  the long term and that they had better  read the fine print of any deal and in  addition the new australian government a  labour government has a much more  progressive foreign policy than the last  one and therefore is telling pacific  pacific island countries what they need  to hear from australia on climate change  which is obviously an existential issue  for them so it is an important way  i'd like to get back to the uh the the  pacific island states and uh there does  appear to be somewhat an appetite for  cooperation with beijing doesn't they i  mean you're talking we have the smaller  arrangement a security arrangement with  the solomon islands a bilateral  agreement with samoa so there is some  sort of an appetite isn't there for  cooperation with beijing  oh look no question i mean these are  countries with enormous development  needs it's understandable that any  country  offering  the loans or the infrastructure or the  technology to help them fill that gap is  going to receive an audience and so  china is part of the solution for the  pacific but it's not the whole solution  and so what we're also seeing  is uh division come to the surface  within countries like solomon islands  for example where uh part of the  community wants closer ties to taiwan  part wants closer ties to beijing part  is worried about the impact of chinese  power and many in that community still  want australia engaged so he's going to  be a a really  multi-coloured tapestry if you like of  foreign assistance to the pacific the  main concern i think from an australian  perspective is that no one country  dominates and it does not use  development as an excuse for a strategic  footprint for security dominance  uh former australian prime minister  scott morrison had referred to the  pacific islands as australia's backyard  did australia drop the ball with  engagement in the region  well  to be fair on the previous government it  was trying to do more uh it had a policy  called the pacific step up where it was  increasing development assistance it was  increasing security assistance and so  forth but the political messaging was  not effective it was not working there  was too much a sense that australia was  either being a little bit condescending  towards small island countries or that  australia was looking at all of their  problems through the prism of u.s china  strategic competition so in fact there's  more continuity than people realize that  the new government i think has got the  tone right  and has for example  made it very clear that we will not use  the word backyard in describing the  pacific instead the pacific is our  neighborhood our region and indeed our  family so talk to us a bit about what  one can expect from this  new government in terms of its  engagement with the pacific island  states  well more broadly pacific uh the broader  indo-pacific asia the new australian  government really has hit the ground  running  we've had a big increase in australia's  national in national security capability  and outlook over the past five years or  so so the new government inherits uh  quite a healthy national security effort  in australia but it will now be much  more engaged diplomatic much more  emphasis on listening on multilateralism  on  broadening the range of countries we're  building ties with not only in the  pacific but also  southeast asia the previous government  rightly elevated the quad of india japan  and the united states as a priority in  australian foreign policy and we will  keep that but the new government will  tie that to the needs of the pacific and  south east asia and we're already seeing  i think some early fruit from that out  of the quad meeting in tokyo last week  where our foreign minister penny wong  implemented a massive increase in aid  spending and helped to encourage greater  quad investment in this region  we leave it there for the time being  thank you so much for joining us today  rory metcalf from the australian  national university thank you so much  thank you
 

